outputs/*.csv
outputs/*.json
outputs/*.joblib
outputs/*.png
outputs/_*.txt
# ml/artifacts/*.joblib is DELIBERATELY NOT ignored below, unlike outputs/.
#
# Deployment audit 2026-08-25. This line used to read `ml/artifacts/*.joblib`
# too, and it swept up the production model bundle along with the training
# scratch it was presumably meant for -- `outputs/` and `ml/artifacts/` are
# different directories with different jobs; only the first is scratch.
# The bundle these nine files make up (pex_regressor.joblib plus a p10/p50/p90
# head for each of three targets) is what `ml/predict.py::MLSurrogate` loads at
# import, and it is genuinely small: 12 MB total, ordinary for git, nowhere
# near LFS territory.
#
# The result: `docs/DEPLOYMENT.md`'s own artifact table said this directory
# was needed at runtime while noting only 32 KB of it was tracked, and nothing
# ever connected those two facts, because nobody had deployed from a clean
# `git clone` until Render did. `GET /api/v1/ml/health` came back
# `"ml_surrogate":"unavailable: FileNotFoundError"` -- the engine ran, the
# XGBoost band models (a separate artifact) loaded, but every ML-surrogate
# prediction was silently falling through to the analytical-only path.
#
# The SHAP png stays ignored -- shap_analysis.py only WRITES it, as a training
# diagnostic; nothing at request time reads it.
ml/artifacts/*.png
__pycache__/
*.pyc
